WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.889
A whispering Batman?

00:00:02.889 --> 00:00:05.100
Inspired by Kurt Cobain?

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.279
In slippers??

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:11.380
Robert Pattinson had some wild ideas about
how to bring Batman to life.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:12.590
Find it all right here.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:18.270
"The Batman" is finally out in theaters, and
there is no one better to play eccentric billionaire

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.750
Bruce Wayne than Robert Pattinson.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:25.630
His version of Batman is less polished and
grungier than previous iterations, and the

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.090
actor has found ways to make the role his
own.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:33.710
He even tried to change a well-known hallmark
attributed to the franchise — Batman's voice.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:39.000
In order to disguise himself from his alter-ego
Bruce, "Batman" films have always included

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.420
some alteration to the character's voice.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:47.780
Be it Christian Bale's trademark gritty roar
or Ben Affleck's voice modulator in the DCEU

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.870
films, changing the voice is an important
aspect of the role.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:56.010
Not to be outdone, Pattinson tried to put
his own flair on the concept, though it didn't

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.730
last for very long.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:02.300
In an attempt to distinguish himself from
his predecessors, Pattinson wanted to steer

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:08.220
away from what audiences had come to know
as Batman and do something "radically different."

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.130
In an interview on "Jimmy Kimmel Live!", he
explained how he tried to change the voice,

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:16.820
"I just thought because everyone does this
kind of gruff, gravelly thing, I'm gonna do

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:17.820
the opposite."

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.870
As for what Pattinson decided his take was
going to be, he told Kimmel,

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:28.590
"I'm going to go really whispery and I tried
to do it for it for the first two weeks and

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.659
it just looked absolutely atrocious."

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:35.799
The actor took his shot and although it didn't
immediately hit with the production of "The

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:38.130
Batman," he eventually found his stride.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:42.500
After his first attempt at a different voice,
he found a happy medium.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.220
Pattinson admitted that something seemingly
happens when you put on the suit.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.299
After he found his groove, he was able to
find the best voice for the film.

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:51.939
"I'm vengeance."

00:01:51.939 --> 00:01:56.560
To Robert Pattinson's credit, what he was
trying wasn't that far off the mark.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.530
With so many versions of Batman, it is only
natural that an actor may want to try something

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:02.530
new.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:04.700
And he wasn't even the first Batman to try
it.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.230
When initially trying on the costume, Pattinson
learned that Batman veteran Christian Bale

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:13.430
had also tried to use a similar intonation
for "Batman Begins."

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:16.790
Bale, or course, portrayed an entirely different
Batman.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:21.680
Where Pattinson's Batman is reclusive and
moody, Bale emphasized Bruce Wayne's wealth

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:22.799
and privilege.

00:02:22.799 --> 00:02:26.900
But even with this dichotomy, it seems that
Pattinson's instincts weren't so different

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.120
from Bale's.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.609
Bale has been somewhat infamous for how dark
and rough his voice became when portraying

00:02:32.609 --> 00:02:33.609
the Batman persona.

00:02:33.609 --> 00:02:38.010
Though he has gotten flak for the voice he
puts on, some fans argue that it's important

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.980
to differentiate Bruce Wayne from Batman.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.779
Wayne is a recognizable figure in Gotham,
a local celebrity, so he would need a severe

00:02:45.779 --> 00:02:49.090
vocal change in order to protect his identity.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:53.330
Pattinson felt comforted that not only did
Bale try to do a different voice, but you

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:57.650
can hear it in a very early teaser trailer
for the 2005 film.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:02.269
You can also hear Bale's early voice experiment
in his audition video.

00:03:02.269 --> 00:03:04.170
"Gotham needs you."

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.390
"And you serve Gotham?"

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:06.390
"I serve justice."

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:10.909
The voice isn't the only difference between
Bale and Pattinson's Batman.

00:03:10.909 --> 00:03:14.799
Director Matt Reeves has been very open about
his influences on the "The Batman."

00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:19.599
In an interview for Empire, the director spoke
about how one of his main influences was Kurt

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:20.599
Cobain.

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:25.049
Nirvana's "Something In the Way" is featured
heavily in the trailer and there is a grungier

00:03:25.049 --> 00:03:28.230
aesthetic present in the promotional materials.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.159
At first Pattinson didn't agree, saying

00:03:30.159 --> 00:03:31.650
"Really?

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:35.230
That's kind of the opposite of what I imagine
Bruce Wayne to be."

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.930
But eventually, he started brainstorming on
the subject.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:42.919
After his idea of the whispering voice failed,
he had some additional input.

00:03:42.919 --> 00:03:45.640
Pattinson joked to Jimmy Kimmel that in the
first costume meeting,

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:51.659
"I wanted to go really, really, really Kurt
Cobain and kind of wanted to wear a dressing

00:03:51.659 --> 00:03:55.430
gown and kind of fluffy bugs bunny slippers."

00:03:55.430 --> 00:04:00.069
But the idea of grunge is still present in
this iteration of "The Batman."

00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:05.109
Even without any whispering or flannel, the
gritty aspect of the film is present in Batman's

00:04:05.109 --> 00:04:07.389
muscle car and beat-up costume.

00:04:07.389 --> 00:04:11.660
And if the reactions to "The Batman" from
fans and critics are any indication, Pattinson

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:15.730
certainly stuck the landing, despite his early
vocal experiments.

